  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The next round of rain showers is expected for \nthe end of the week, though drought relief is unlikely. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure to the south will build through mid-week, bringing\ndry conditions until late week. Low pressure to the northwest in\nCanada tracks eastward, bringing a relatively weak cold front\nover the state on Friday. Pressure gradient between the high \npressure and the approaching low could help produce some breezy\nsouthwest winds Thursday afternoon into Friday. \n\nBased on global models, PWATs are roughly 1.25-1.75 inches, \nwith higher values concentrated around the central and Downeast \nregions. Models currently hint at precipitation generally moving\nin late Thursday night from the northwest, and moving to the \nsoutheast by Friday mid-morning to afternoon. There is still \nsome model discrepancies in the exact timing, however. Global \nensembles generally keep precipitation totals low (under a third\nof an inch). Some members, though, do show a narrow band of \nenhanced precipitation, near an inch, across the state, though \nlocation is highly uncertain. Continue to monitor the forecast \nfor updates, as models begin to get a better handle on the \natmospheric set up.",
